#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166752 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a6b6e1-fb99-5ebe-9f3f-000878f79f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector With Respect to a Given Basis in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b7570ab-fd63-531d-8e02-29bf746ab1db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559280} {:answer/id #uuid "933e3d56-7dd8-5f4b-8baf-cfed5b9e1537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559382} {:answer/id #uuid "9580e7d9-2197-512f-98b3-2771aef78be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559388} {:answer/id #uuid "9baad326-2a3f-5a1a-891d-cfc23346b6ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559411} {:answer/id #uuid "c2dcc144-7c34-56a2-8495-45807a3fe82c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2dcc144-7c34-56a2-8495-45807a3fe82c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559496} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dd1c54a-c831-5cbe-8eef-ef06db6fc42c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ade0c9c-871d-5f78-8adc-bcd701d0727a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106750" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 & 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-10 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -20 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-10 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}=-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second  equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-4.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2(-4) & =-10 \\\\ -{x}_{1} & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2+(-4)=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162130 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b8dd2d-ecaa-58bf-baa3-72dc8f37523f" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Joint CDF Geometrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b969c54c-bfd6-5745-87c4-05539c2d858d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159711} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7892de-03c9-534c-b80a-fbfa7d7b67bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4} - \\frac{y}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159712} {:answer/id #uuid "34019b57-f57b-5b58-ae30-9c2fbf9dcf91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159713} {:answer/id #uuid "348fb3b8-e710-5dbe-80e3-014de77c13af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159714} {:answer/id #uuid "44af18f0-e53d-572d-b06f-c94dcdd9471e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{y}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159715}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f37bb7-7f21-51c2-b7ae-c6c13364b14c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159710}] :question/id #uuid "d2641b70-6005-5dd7-a26d-e0feed529994" :question/math-academy-id "q-145888" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x, y)$, where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$S_{X} = {1, 2, 3, 4}, S_{Y} = {0, 1, 2, 3}$.\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x > 4, y > 3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e593685-f5ca-5501-94bf-7cb4d49e935b" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Involving Reciprocals and Radicals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00ed170e-f9ef-581a-a28f-e895638d17f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}\\ne -1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435776} {:answer/id #uuid "6dfe18bb-7b8e-5e23-b6bb-c3d39e3896af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy-$plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435782} {:answer/id #uuid "a0dbcdf0-68f4-5a84-9587-f3fa35d6aba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}=-1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435790} {:answer/id #uuid "dcdecbfa-f590-53c6-baa7-254b9858e4a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435795} {:answer/id #uuid "f87318a8-3643-5bd8-8b57-84f2fc51ba95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}>-1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00ed170e-f9ef-581a-a28f-e895638d17f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}\\ne -1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435776} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3b045b8-bf15-51b1-816f-a65dbb6c4b2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8d614db-1f17-5013-b1ed-45363a374722" :question/math-academy-id "q-158552" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+1\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{3}\\ne -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{3}\\ne -1\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124534 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d288b96-5588-5a61-8707-1cd7320722ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351675} {:answer/id #uuid "320557e4-68a2-5c84-980d-3b3ee616da1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352030} {:answer/id #uuid "37b6a32d-8f94-5003-8bc3-b248e652ca30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352083} {:answer/id #uuid "53daa546-f7c8-5488-9b6d-80860ccb8bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352314} {:answer/id #uuid "f8491c4f-7494-58c4-8f86-8a6e40cbd288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d288b96-5588-5a61-8707-1cd7320722ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351675} :answer-field/id #uuid "835f08c2-4c07-5aef-87e1-fbe4a54164d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ad91747-58ce-5cb7-bde7-896fa9da6f06" :question/math-academy-id "q-84607" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ 1 & 0 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the third column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nSo, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{3}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations whose augmented matrix $M$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix is already  in row echelon form.  It has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.  From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}(-2(\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3})-3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"}]}
